<TopicPage slug="subjects/criminology/foundational/changing-awareness-of-crime">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Unit 1 of the WJEC Level 3 Applied Diploma in Criminology starts from an uncomfortable
    observation: <strong>what the public "knows" about crime is mostly manufactured</strong> —
    by what gets reported, what gets recorded, and what the media chooses to dramatise. Some
    crimes dominate the headlines; others are so hidden, so normalised, or so "complex" that
    nobody reports them at all. This lesson walks the unit's first four assessment criteria:
    the types of crime (AC1.1), why some go unreported and what that costs (AC1.2–1.3), how
    media represents crime (AC1.4), and how we even measure the thing (AC1.6).
  </p>
  <p>
    A single example shows why the unit opens here. In England and Wales, fraud — defrauding
    individuals and organisations — has for years been among the <em>most commonly
    experienced</em> offence types in the national victimisation survey, running to millions of
    incidents a year; yet ask anyone to picture "crime" and they picture burglary or violence.
    The mismatch is not stupidity. It is the product of reporting rates (most fraud is never
    brought to the police), of recording systems (online fraud crossing force boundaries fits
    police counting awkwardly), and of media selection (a mugging makes a story; a
    phishing email makes a paragraph). "Changing awareness of crime" means learning to see the
    machinery that produces the picture — because policy, policing priorities and personal
    precaution are all set by the picture, not the reality.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "crime" is not a fixed list handed down by nature. What
  counts as a crime is a legal category that changes with time and place — same-sex acts were
  crimes in Britain until 1967; witchcraft was once a capital offence; corporate practices
  legal in one jurisdiction are crimes in another. The unit's job is precisely to keep the
  <em>legal</em>, <em>social</em> and <em>publicly imagined</em> boundaries of crime separate
  in your mind.</p>

  <h2>Types of crime you must analyse</h2>
  <p>
    AC1.1 names a set of crime types, and the unit requires you to analyse each along the same
    four axes: what criminal offence (if any) is committed, who the victims are, who the
    offenders are, and — the axis that ties Unit 1 together — the <strong>level of public
    awareness</strong>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>White-collar crime</strong> — committed by the respectable in the course of work: organised, corporate and professional variants. Fraud, tax evasion, price-fixing, and corporate negligence causing harm. Edwin Sutherland coined the term in 1939 precisely to show that crime is not concentrated among the poor. Often deviant and criminal at once, and routinely underestimated because its victims are diffuse — thousands of small losses with no single outraged victim.</li>
    <li><strong>Moral crimes</strong> — acts against perceived standards of decency: prostitution, vagrancy, illegal gambling, assisted suicide, under-age drinking. "Victimless" is contested, and that contest drives whether anyone calls the police — and whether they should be crimes at all is a live, legitimate debate the unit expects you to argue, not assume.</li>
    <li><strong>State crimes</strong> — offences by or with the acquiescence of governments: genocide, torture, imprisonment without trial, human-rights violations, state corporate crime. The offender writes the rules, so awareness depends on outsiders — journalists, whistleblowers, international bodies — which makes the awareness question existential rather than academic.</li>
    <li><strong>Technological (e-)crime</strong> — hacking, phishing, identity theft, online fraud, malware. Borderless, fast-moving, and frequently never detected by the victim until long after; offences may fall under the Computer Misuse Act 1990 or ordinary fraud statutes, and many incidents are never reported because reporting seems hopeless.</li>
    <li><strong>Individual crimes</strong> — hate crime, honour crime, domestic abuse: offences against a person precisely because of who they are or what relationship they are in, typically under-reported for deeply personal reasons — dependence on the offender, shame, fear of not being believed, or loyalty to family.</li>
    <li><strong>Domestic abuse</strong> deserves its own attention as the unit's recurring case study: criminal across several statutes, historically treated as private, transformed over fifty years by feminist campaigning, dedicated legislation (from the Domestic Violence and Matrimonial Proceedings Act 1976 to the Domestic Abuse Act 2021) and compulsory recording — a before-and-after demonstration that <em>awareness is manufactured and can be re-manufactured</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The spread of public awareness across these types is enormous. Domestic abuse, after
    decades of campaigns and legal reform, is now loudly visible even though reporting remains
    partial; corporate fraud may cost the economy billions yet barely registers as "crime" in
    the public mind, because its victims are scattered shareholders and its offender a
    respectable logo. Awareness is not a by-product of harm; it is manufactured by reporting,
    recording and media — which is exactly why the next two sections matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Why crimes go unreported — and what that does</h2>
  <p>
    The unit divides the reasons in two. <strong>Personal reasons</strong>: fear (of the
    offender, or of not being believed), shame, disinterest, or feeling not really affected —
    "it was only a phone". <strong>Social and cultural reasons</strong>: lack of knowledge that
    a crime occurred or was a crime (a scam that looks like bad luck; coercion that looks like
    a relationship), complexity ("who do I even tell about a frozen crypto account?"), lack of
    media interest, lack of current public concern — and culture-bound crimes such as honour
    killing or accusations of witchcraft, where reporting would betray the community itself.
    Each reason predicts a different remedy: fear responds to victim support and anonymity;
    ignorance responds to campaigns; complexity responds to specialised reporting channels —
    which is how Action Fraud and the NPCC's True Vision (for hate crime) came to exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequences cut both ways. Unreported crime breeds a <em>ripple effect</em> (one
    offender's harm spreads through victims, families, neighbourhoods), lets behaviour
    <em>decriminalise</em> in practice, skews <em>police prioritisation</em>, and swells the
    pool of <em>unrecorded crime</em>. But the unit also asks for the positives: silence can
    precede <em>cultural change</em> (what was shamed becomes reportable — as with domestic
    abuse), and under-reporting can drive <em>legal and procedural change</em> once the dark
    figure is finally exposed. Worked through the domestic abuse case: the dark figure was
    exposed by surveys and campaigns in the 1970s–90s; awareness rose; reporting rose —
    and the recorded numbers <em>went up</em>, which is the crucial subtlety. A rising crime
    statistic can mean rising crime <em>or rising confidence to report</em>, and confusing the
    two is the standard error this unit exists to prevent.
  </p>

  <h2>The media's version of crime</h2>
  <p>
    Newspapers, television, film, electronic gaming, social media and music all portray
    crime — fictionally and factually — and the portrayal is systematically unrepresentative:
    violent and sexual offences are over-shown, the common crimes under-shown. Content
    analyses going back to the 1970s (Chibnall's work on British crime reporting) found
    recurring news values — immediacy, dramatisation, personalisation, and the structural
    violence of "a good story" — that distort the picture independent of any editor's
    intention. Stanley Cohen's <em>Folk Devils and Moral Panics</em> (1972) supplies the
    classic mechanism: exaggerated reporting creates a folk devil (the 1960s Mods and Rockers
    were his case), public concern spikes, and responses and punishments escalate beyond what
    the trend justifies — and once the label exists, more deviance is noticed, reported and
    created, completing the loop. Media representation feeds directly into perceptions of
    crime trends, stereotyping of offenders, and changing priorities — which is why AC1.5 asks
    you to explain the impact with theory, not anecdote. The modern extension: social media
    shortens the panic cycle to days, lets unverified clips stand in for statistics, and —
    as the 2011 England riots showed — can organise as well as amplify.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring the unmeasurable</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the measurement gap, step by step — why no single number can be 'the' crime rate" widget="equationStep" config={gapCfg} />
  <p>
    The widget walks the arithmetic of the <strong>dark figure</strong>. Of 100 real incidents,
    roughly 40 reach the police as reports — the Crime Survey for England and Wales has found
    for decades that only around four in ten of the crimes its respondents experience are ever
    brought to police attention. Of those, recording rules and evidence tests shave the
    official count down further (perhaps 35). The 60 that never got past the victim are
    invisible to every table built from police data. This is why the field uses two
    instruments with deliberately different biases. <strong>Police-recorded crime</strong> is
    an administrative count: complete for murder (there are no unreported murders for long),
    sensitive to recording practice and priorities, and needed for local operational data.
    The <strong>Crime Survey for England and Wales</strong> asks tens of thousands of
    households directly about victimisation, reported or not: it is immune to recording-rule
    changes and captures the dark figure — but it misses "victimless" crimes, businesses and
    children under 16 (in the main sample), and it inherits survey error like any
    questionnaire. Neither is "the true rate"; each has its own reliability, validity and
    ethics (AC1.6's evaluation criteria). Read them together, or not at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because both instruments are flawed in different directions, criminologists triangulate.
    The two-hundred-year history of trying is itself a lesson in measurement: each milestone
    below created a better lens and a new kind of blind spot.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="two centuries of trying to count crime in England and Wales" widget="timeline" config={measureTimeline} />

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements separate a strong analysis from a competent one. First,
    <strong>recording reform as a false trend</strong>: the 2002 National Crime Recording
    Standard moved forces from counting what they judged provable toward counting what victims
    said happened; recorded violence against the person rose sharply in the following years
    with no matching rise in the survey — the cleanest demonstration in British statistics
    that the recording instrument changed while the underlying reality did not. In 2014 an
    inspection (HMIC) judged police recording unacceptably poor, which is another direction of
    the same lesson: administrative data measures the <em>administration</em>. Second, the
    <strong>gap is not uniform</strong>: reporting rates for vandalism are far higher than for
    fraud or sexual offences, so the dark figure is a shadow with a shape — and the shape tells
    you where interventions should aim. Third, the <strong>open frontier</strong>: much e-crime
    now bypasses both instruments — fraud reported to banks is counted in neither
    police-recorded statistics nor, until recently, the survey's main counts, so the fastest-
    growing crime category in Western societies is also the least measured. The unit's method
    — always ask which instrument, what its blind spot is, and who benefits from the number
    that gets quoted — transfers to every statistic you will ever be shown.
  </p>
  <p>
    A closing worked exercise rehearses that method on a claim you might read tomorrow:
    <em>"shoplifting has doubled since 2015"</em>. The disciplined questions, in order: which
    instrument says so — police-recorded theft or the survey (whose coverage of shop theft is
    weak, since businesses are outside the household sample)? Did recording practice change
    (post-2014 forces were pushed to record more, and several retailers began systematically
    reporting rather than absorbing losses)? What does the denominator do — retail moved
    toward self-scan checkouts with different loss profiles? And who is quoting it — a
    retailers' association, a police force seeking resources, a newspaper in a slow news
    week? None of this means the trend is false; organised shop theft genuinely rose in many
    jurisdictions. It means the claim's confidence should be calibrated to its instrument —
    and that calibrating, reflexively, is precisely the skill AC1.1–AC1.6 were designed to
    build: awareness of crime that includes awareness of how crime comes to be known at all.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WJEC Level 3 Applied Diploma in Criminology, Unit 1 (AC1.1–AC1.6), 2022 specification; ONS, Crime Survey for England and Wales methodology; Home Office, National Crime Recording Standard (2002); HMIC, Crime-recording inspection (2014); Cohen, Folk Devils and Moral Panics (1972); Chibnall, Law-and-Order News (1977); Sutherland, "White-Collar Criminality" (1939).</p>
</TopicPage>
